diff --git a/doc/filters.texi b/doc/filters.texi
index 3534c87..e7d7434 100644
--- a/doc/filters.texi
+++ b/doc/filters.texi
@@ -16702,6 +16702,92 @@ ffmpeg -i input.mov -vf lensfun=make=Canon:model="Canon EOS 100D":lens_model="Ca
 Low Complexity Enhancement Video Codec filter based on liblcevc_dec
 (@url{https://github.com/v-novaltd/LCEVCdec}).
 
+@section libhdrgrading
+
+Grade SDR video into HDR with libhdrgrading
+(@url{https://github.com/zeriyoshi/libhdrgrading}): the frames come out as
+single-layer PQ (SMPTE ST 2084) over BT.2020, brighter where the content
+allows (light sources, highlights) while the diffuse surfaces stay close to
+the SDR's. Each output frame carries the mastering display (Display P3 up to
+the rendition ceiling) and its own HDR10+ and SMPTE ST 2094-50 metadata.
+
+The statistics a frame is graded on average over a window of the frames of
+its scene, so the filter holds that many frames (@option{future}) before
+sending one on. The window, @samp{recursive} and @option{fps} need a
+libhdrgrading built with its temporal features (off by default); without
+them each frame is graded on its own statistics and those options are
+refused.
+
+To enable compilation of this filter you need to configure FFmpeg with
+@code{--enable-libhdrgrading}.
+
+The filter accepts the following options:
+
+@table @option
+@item preset
+The content's preset: @samp{photo} (photographs and live action, the
+default), @samp{anime} or @samp{game}.
+
+@item temporal
+How the statistics follow the frames: @samp{windowed} (the mean over a window
+of the scene, the default), @samp{recursive} (an exponential moving average,
+no latency) or @samp{static} (one fixed mapping, no statistics).
+
+@item latency
+The latency profile: @samp{offline} (converting a file, the default),
+@samp{playback} (watched, keeping up on average) or @samp{low} (each frame
+within its interval, no frames ahead).
+
+@item past
+@item future
+The frames before and after a frame its statistics average over (-1: the
+library's defaults; @option{future} defaults to @option{past}).
+
+@item proxy
+The longest side of the image the frames are analyzed on under the realtime
+effort (the default of the @samp{playback} and @samp{low} latencies; the
+quality effort analyzes at full size; 0: full size). Default value is @code{960}.
+
+@item w
+@item h
+The output size, through the library's scaler; one side 0 keeps the aspect
+ratio. Default is the input's.
+
+@item fps
+The output frame rate; the frames between the input's are interpolated.
+Default is the input's.
+
+@item threads
+The library's threads (0: one per core). Default value is @code{0}.
+
+@item gpu
+What runs on the GPU: @samp{off} (the default), @samp{auto} (the filters,
+the conversion and the grading) or @samp{filters} (the filters only).
+
+@item flash_limit
+Limit the harmful flashes the grading would make stronger (ITU-R BT.1702-3).
+Enabled by default.
+
+@item dither
+Dither the output. Enabled by default.
+
+@item settings
+Any of the library's settings by their names, after the preset, as a list of
+@var{name}=@var{value} pairs separated by @samp{:} (e.g.
+@code{diffuse-max=1.5:peak-nits=1000}).
+@end table
+
+The output is @code{yuv420p10}, @code{p010} or @code{rgb48}.
+
+@subsection Examples
+@itemize
+@item
+Grade an SDR file into 10-bit HEVC:
+@example
+ffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf libhdrgrading=preset=anime -c:v libx265 -pix_fmt yuv420p10le out.mkv
+@end example
+@end itemize
+
